\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Ukraine's c.bank sells dollars for eighth day  \b0\par\par\uc2 307 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 14:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (adds details)  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, March 10 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the eighth day on Friday, selling dollars at 5.06 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is not calming down. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to come out again," said a dealer from a foreign bank in Kiev.  \par \par \uc2 He said strong demand from importers and slow but steady sales of government debt by non-residents remained among the key factors behind the move.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent around $120 million in its near daily \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far in March as the demand for hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been fuelled by increasing political uncertainty ahead of a parliamentary elections on March 26.  \par \par \uc2 Anatoly Shapovalov, first deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chairman, said earlier this week there was no reason to speak about capital flight and said he saw no threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the results of the election emerge and the first indication of the country's future government appear.  \par \par \uc2 Ukraine is electing a parliament this month with more powers and the right to nominate a prime minister and most ministers.  \par \par \uc2 Opinion polls have shown so far that none of the 45 parties running for parliament will be able to win an outright majority and coalition talks will be required.  \par \par \uc2 The pro-Russian party Regions of Ukraine, led by Viktor Yanukovich, who was defeated by President Viktor Yushchenko in the polls in 2004, is leading in the opinion polls.  \par \par \uc2 The pro-president Our Ukraine and a bloc led by former Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko, once a presidential ally but now a rival, are the second and the third.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 UKRAINE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|BNX|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060310e23a00122\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
