\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Ukraine's c. bank sells dollars for 9th day  \b0\par\par\uc2 293 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2006\par 12:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Jan 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday as part of long-running efforts to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling dollars at 5.06 for the ninth consecutive trading day, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened on the interbank market in mid-day trading to 5.055/5.062 against 5.061/5.0695 at the opening.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly daily to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  against political turbulence as Ukraine heads into a hotly-contested March 26 parliamentary election.  \par \par \uc2 Ukraine has been gripped by a constitutional crisis since parliament dismissed the government in response to the conclusion of a deal to import gas from Russia at sharply increased prices.  \par \par \uc2 President Viktor Yushchenko has refused to recognise parliament's move. Parliament resumes its sittings on Feb. 7.  \par \par \uc2 Anatoly Shapovalov, first deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chairman, told Reuters this week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had spent about $780 million from reserves to prop up the sliding \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Tensions have calmed this week as parliament is in recess until February 7.  \par \par \uc2 Economists are also worried over uncertainty on Russian natural gas supplies to Ukraine this year.  \par \par \uc2 Ukraine and Russia have agreed on gas deliveries for this year, raising the price to $95 per 1,000 cubic meters against $50 previously. But the signing of the agreement to seal the deal has been postponed amid wrangling over legal details.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue defending a level of 5.06 to the dollar in the near term until the government announces new macroeconomic forecasts for this year.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 UKRAINE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|BNX|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060127e21r0011t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
