\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Ukraine's c. bank says ready to back  \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalya Zinets  \par \uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2006\par 12:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (adds quotes)  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Jan 19 (Reuters) - The slide in Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is linked to politics not economics and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to prop it, Anatoly Shapovalov, first deputy to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chairman, said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Shapovalov told Reuters the bank was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 5.06 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar, the rate it has been defending since August, but would not cover speculative demand.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to be present on the market in the coming days and continue its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  at longstanding rates," he said in a telephone interview.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no need to change the rate for now. The major reason for destabilisation is not economic but political, namely a standoff, election campaigning."  \par \par \uc2 Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  <UAH=> dipped to a new 9-month low on Thursday amid political uncertainty following parliament's dismissal of the government last week -- less than two months before a March 26 parliamentary election.  \par \par \uc2 Shapovalov also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could revise its \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast for this year if the government undertook major changes to its macroeconomic forecasts for 2006. The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  average rate is targetted at 5.00-5.20 per dollar for 2006.  \par \par \uc2 "The government has not reviewed its macroeconomic indicators, they are being worked out. We will look at the agreement which is to be signed on gas. We believe there are no reasons for major changes," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "We will stick to the policy approved earlier. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will help with its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to avoid any sharp moves."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Russia signed a protocol on a new gas deal on Jan. 4.  \par \par \uc2 Kiev is to start paying $95 per 1,000 cubic meters of gas in 2006 compared with $50 previously, with purchases conducted through an intermediary. A more detailed agreement is expected to be signed this week.  \par \par \uc2 Parliament, whose members have long been focused on the March election campaign, sacked the liberal government of President Viktor Yushchenko on grounds that the gas deal betrayed the national interest.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-UKRAINE-BANK (UPDATE 1, INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|BNX  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060119e21j000ts\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
