\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Ukraine's c. bank sells dollars for third day  \b0\par\par\uc2 399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2006\par 13:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (adds quotes)  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Jan 13 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday, selling dollars at 5.06 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the third day running, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  <UAH=> plunged to a 9-month low this week after parliament dismissed the government with little more than two months to go until a parliamentary election.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered to about 5.065/5.075 to the dollar on Friday due to continued \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support, dealers said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  sank to 5.0950/5.1050 per dollar, its lowest since April 2005, on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "Today the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained ground. But the situation has not changed in principle. Hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply on the market has increased only slightly and we are unlikely to manage without the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's help," a dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hit by parliament's no-confidence vote on Tuesday against the government, which triggered a constitutional clash with President Viktor Yushchenko, who has refused to recognise it as legitimate.  \par \par \uc2 Yushchenko said he would keep Premier Yuri Yekhanurov in office until the March 26 election and vowed to uphold stability. But the market was split over the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's future.  \par \par \uc2 Valery Lytvytsky, a senior adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chairman, said there were no reasons for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility due to robust \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and an expected trade surplus.  \par \par \uc2 "We have funds to cover demand (for hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ). And there will be no high demand anyway. The current account is strong, the budget deficit is no higher than last year's," he told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves grew to about $19.4 billion at the end of 2005 compared with $9.5 billion a year ago. The 2006 budget targets a deficit of 2.5 percent of gross domestic product compared with a 2.3 percent deficit in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 But Oleksander Suhonyako, president of Ukraine's Banking Association, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  could slump to about 5.2/dollar as consumers would buy up dollars due to growing instability ahead of the vote.  \par \par \uc2 Many Ukrainians lost their life savings in failed monetary reforms in the early 1990s and have since viewed the dollar as a safe haven in times of political turbulence.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 UKRAINE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060113e21d000xt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
