\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Fitch cuts Ukraine credit outlook on political row  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2006\par 11:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with analyst comment, background, fresh prices)  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 12 (Reuters) - Fitch Ratings lowered its credit rating outlook on Ukraine to stable from positive on Thursday but affirmed the ratings at 'BB-' after parliament voted this week to sack the government over a murky gas deal with Russia.  \par \par \uc2 Fitch's decision is the first negative sign on Ukraine's credit rating since the country agreed the pricing deal last week, doubling the price paid by Kiev after a standoff in which Moscow cut off supplies.  \par \par \uc2 "Fitch believes that the formation of the next government could be a prolonged affair and that the outcome may be a weak coalition vulnerable to splits," Sharon Raj, sovereign ratings analyst at Fitch in London, said in a statement.  \par \par \uc2 "Against this background, the outlook for progress with structural reform has worsened, while the risk of a slow or inappropriate policy response to the recent gas price or any future shock has increased," she added.  \par \par \uc2 President Viktor Yushchenko has refused to recognise Tuesday's no-confidence vote as legitimate and wants to keep Premier Yuri Yekhuranov in office until parliamentary elections due on March 26.  \par \par \uc2 Ukraine's benchmark sovereign dollar bond due 2013 fell on the Fitch news, losing about one point in price before clawing back to a loss of just 0.375 points at 106.625 to yield 6.589 percent <UAHGLB13=RR>.  \par \par \uc2 Ukraine's external bonds, which have a scarcity premium, are not expected to be hammered lower in any dramatic fashion as investors wait for clearer direction on domestic politics and the impact of the gas deal on the economy.  \par \par \uc2 Ukraine's sovereign debt traded at 192 basis points over U.S. Treasuries on the benchmark JP Morgan Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus <11EMJ> <.JPMEMBIPLUS>, five basis points wider on the day, indicating an increase in investor risk aversion.  \par \par \uc2 "I think there really are significant economic and political risks for certain but the credit continues to trade very tight," said Frank Gill, emerging markets analyst at IDEAglobal in London.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , already weak, showed little reaction to the Fitch news. \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday to sell dollars at 5.06 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  <UAH=>, and said it would satisfy all bids at that level, after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped to a nine-month low around 5.09.  \par \par \uc2 But on Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was around 5.095 in early trade before creeping back to 5.0805, a 0.40 percent loss on the day.  \par \par \uc2 SMALL DEBT  \par \par \uc2 Ukraine's relatively small debt to gross domestic product ratio and a tradition of current account surpluses, which supported its bonds and ratings, are now considered under threat as a result of the gas agreement.  \par \par \uc2 "Right now, potential investors are going to hold back until they can determine what sort of government is going to be voted in in March," Gill said.  \par \par \uc2 "If we see a lot of political gridlock, it is probably negative for foreign investment in the medium term."  \par \par \uc2 Rival ratings agency Standard & Poor's affirmed its Ukraine ratings on Wednesday and kept its outlook at stable. S&P rates Ukraine 'BB-', the same as Fitch, while Moody's Investors Service is one notch lower at 'B1'.  \par \par \uc2 S&P said despite relatively bleak economic prospects, Ukraine still enjoyed a fairly robust external position and low debt levels, giving the government flexibility with its budget.  \par \par \uc2 The parliamentary revolt was led by former Prime Minister Yulia Tymoshenko and analysts said the opposition was using anger over the gas deal to undermine Yushckenko's supporters ahead of the March elections, which will bring broader powers to whoever has the majority.  \par \par \uc2 Fitch said the vote may have minimal near-term consequences for policy.  \par \par \uc2 "While Ukraine's long-term growth potential remains strong, 2006 will be a tough year and any ongoing political weaknesses will compound the economic challenges," the statement said.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-UKRAINE-FITCH (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|BNX  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060112e21c000q9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
