\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine \highlight\uc2  c. bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  first time since Nov 25  \b0\par\par\uc2 303 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 2005\par 14:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, more quotes)  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Dec 22 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday, buying dollars at 5.00 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since November 25, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "The market was balanced in recent weeks. Today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was caused by a temporary increase in dollar supply. Virtually, there was only one large seller," a dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  <UAH=> rose to 5.008/5012 per dollar in late trade from 5.03/5.035 to the dollar at the opening.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last purchased dollars on November 25, when it was buying out dollars at a rate of 5.0 to the dollar from the sale of the country's largest steel mill Kryvorizhstal.  \par \par \uc2 Kryvorizhstal was sold in October to the world's largest steel producer Mittal Steel for about $4.8 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the market was likely to be volatile in the New Year due to worsening foreign trade figures and growing political uncertainty ahead of a parliamentary elections due in March, 2006.  \par \par \uc2 "Looking at our macroeconomic forecasts, I do not have much certainty. They are not going to sell 'a new Kryvorizhstal' in the coming month or two, elections are close and we have an unsatisfactory export and import balance," a dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts have said there is a chance Ukraine's export-led economy could experience no growth next year due to deteriorating world markets and a likely rise in gas prices.  \par \par \uc2 The economy ministry also said on Thursday Ukraine is expected to post a trade deficit of $477 million in 2006 compared to an expected surplus of $863 million this year.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 UKRAINE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051222e1cm001e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
